## The UCRT stdio options (ImplementationPlan.md 5.15.2 'The UCRT printf
## family and stdio options (windows-msvc)', hosted-libc H3; 5.9.13's
## Resolutions bullet as the section 12 row of 2026-10-28 amends it; M26.d,
## M26.f). On x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil, when the link defines
## __local_stdio_printf_options (resp. __local_stdio_scanf_options), the
## module initializer ORs the options bits into the word that function
## returns, after the relocations and before the constructors, per defined
## function
##   call native int <f>(); dup; ldind.i8; ldc.i8 <bits>; or; stind.i8
## with PrintfBits 0x24 (LEGACY_WIDE_SPECIFIERS | STANDARD_ROUNDING) and
## ScanfBits 0x2 by default (the UCRT's legacy wide-specifier mode, the
## default). A link that names legacy_stdio_float_rounding clears 0x20 from
## PrintfBits; one that names iso_stdio_wide_specifiers (the opt-out of
## _CRT_STDIO_ISO_WIDE_SPECIFIERS) clears 0x4 from PrintfBits and 0x2 from
## ScanfBits; legacy_stdio_wide_specifiers selects the default mode and,
## like legacy_stdio_definitions, changes nothing (section 12 row '5.15.2's
## startup options corrected (wide specifiers)'). The four names, given by
## -l, by an embedded /DEFAULTLIB: request or by #pragma comment(lib) (which
## clang writes as '/DEFAULTLIB:<name>.lib'), with or without .lib, are C
## runtime libraries that msil-ld ignores: it never searches for them (no
## such file exists here), and --verbose notes
##   ignoring C runtime library '-l<name>'
##   ignoring C runtime library request '<name>' from <file>
## Opts.py (below) prints the initializer's calls in IL order, the options
## pattern as 'options <f> |= 0x<bits>'. Cases:
## - an object that defines both functions as COMDAT copies (as every TU
##   that includes <stdio.h> does), one that defines only the printf one,
##   one that defines neither, and the same definitions on linux-gnu, where
##   nothing is applied;
## - an LTO link of bitcode whose only definitions and calls of both
##   functions are noinline linkonce_odr (as <stdio.h>'s inline functions):
##   msil-ld keeps both definitions visible to regular objects, so they stay
##   global definitions of the LTO object (map file), their reads are not
##   folded (a helper still calls them), and the image applies the bits as
##   the object link does; --lto-emit-asm text defines them and its
##   initializer applies the same bits as the image, the link's C runtime
##   names included (msil-ld passes them to the backend as module flags,
##   section 12 row '5.15.2's startup options amended (linked text)'); the
##   same bitcode on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil gets no visibility
##   exception, so LTO internalizes both functions (they are not global
##   symbols of the link) and nothing is applied;
## - archive members as the toolchain's helper archive holds them
##   (msil_ucrt_stdio.c, msil_ucrt_stdio_options.c; every definition weak):
##   a program that declares printf without <stdio.h> extracts the printf
##   member and, through it, the options member, and the printf bits apply
##   (under the default --gc-sections the unused __local_stdio_scanf_options
##   is removed and gets none; with --no-gc-sections both get theirs); a link
##   that mixes the member's weak printf with another object's COMDAT printf
##   and __local_stdio_printf_options (as every TU that includes <stdio.h>
##   and calls printf defines them) links without a diagnostic in either
##   order, with one method of each name and the printf bits applied once.

